{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1234 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "77e25b3d39b52aab078a8a5bbb65461a147f366ef2c658dc142db028ff963d88" :edb/db-before-t 1233 :edb/db-after-t 1234 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534546 50 #inst "2026-10-12T02:37:58.287Z" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186124437 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425118 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425119 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186124439 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425120 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266679 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266969 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267491 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268013 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268390 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425099 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425100 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425101 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425102 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425106 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268476 1078 #edb/ref 17592186268390 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186268476 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425106 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268885 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186268885 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=e.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(e,2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(e,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(e,2) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(e,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(e,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =1(y-2) \\\\ z & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(e,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=e \\\\ z=y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336656 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336740 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186338001 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186338031 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186338146 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425107 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425108 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425110 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425111 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186338374 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425114 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186338374 1078 #edb/ref 17592186336656 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186338374 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425110 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186338913 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186338913 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 false] [17592186425089 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425121 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425089 1043 #edb/ref 175921864251