86425115 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425092 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425115 1079 #uuid "472a8778-3d0c-5974-8ff7-bc1d5d8b12f2" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425090 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425096 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425097 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425109 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425113 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1077 "selection" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425090 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425116 1079 #uuid "4d50699f-3ed7-5626-8b63-c6ce285403be" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425091 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425094 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425098 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425103 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425104 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1077 "selection" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425098 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425117 1079 #uuid "9d7802a6-4174-5690-9124-8f551b3466e5" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425116 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425118 1069 "q-64966" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425118 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={2}^{x+y}y(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425118 1072 #uuid "5cb4c36d-f79b-5525-8cf7-91f3e27f40c2" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425118 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(0,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={2}^{0+1}(1)(0\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425117 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425119 1069 "q-71228" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425119 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)-(x+y)^{3}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425119 1072 #uuid "666541af-0f77-5a3b-95c7-e3f70c0a6b57" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425119 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)-(1+0)^{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =-1(y) \\\\ z+1 & =-y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425115 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425120 1069 "q-71226" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425120 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=5.$ Given that the point $P(4,5,-2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425120 1072 #uuid "d880e565-bd50-5672-98e1-45142968e59d" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425120 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(4,5).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(4,5) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}{2(4)-(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(4,5) & ={f}_{x}(4,5)(x-4) \\\\ z-(-2) & =1(x-4) \\\\ z+2 & =x-4 \\\\ z & =x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,5,-2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5 \\\\ z=x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=5,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-6$ (or $x-z-6=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it." 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425121 1062 #uuid "0dfd5f35-4d13-5e8b-b12c-5286da4ef943" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425121 1063 #edb/ref 17592186268885 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425122 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425125 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425122 1062 #uuid "37de0ec1-dde9-55e5-b218-6452fee1d909" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425122 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425118 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425123 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425124 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425123 1062 #uuid "a385dd67-f4d9-5c4e-9dcb-07bacb547fb1" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425123 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425120 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425124 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425121 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425124 1062 #uuid "a445cd29-271b-5b63-aa35-8be905e25a57" 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425124 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425119 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425125 1058 #edb/ref 17592186425123 13194139534546 17592186045417 true] [17592186425125 1062 #uuid "dc115997-e2